    "q-214270": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214270",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A$ containing all possible trapezoids in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∼y\n$$\n\nRecall that $x∼y$ means that $x$ and $y$ are similar.\n\nWhich of the following are true?\n\n| The relation is reflexive | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| The relation is symmetric | {{field-2}} |\n| The relation  is transitive | {{field-3}} |\n| The relation is an equivalence relation | {{field-4}} |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is reflexive. For all $x\\in A,$ we have $x∼x.$ Every trapezoid is similar with itself.\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is symmetric. For all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x∼y,$ then $y∼x.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of similarity transformations in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x∼y),$ then we can always find a sequence of similarity transformations that maps $y$ to $x.$\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is transitive. For all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x∼y,$ and $y∼z,$ then $x∼z.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of similarity transformations in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x∼y)$ and another sequence that maps $y$ to $z,$ then we can always find a sequence that maps $x$ to $z.$\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
